Subject: [PATCH 08/12] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's series 5, patch 8;
 built on 5c7abd72a with the ten patches of series 4, the move of the old
 front pages and the first seven of series 5 applied): EVERY STEP UP OF A
 CEILING NEEDS ITS RECORD (main's register row P-816, found by main reading
 series 2; its B1893). The rule of a ceiling compared two states: the file at
 the rule's anchor and the file now. A ceiling taken down and then put back up
 to where it stood is no higher now than at the anchor, and the step up was
 read by nothing. Main's two cases: the identification baseline taken from 1
 to 0 and back to 1 with no record, and the gate said ok; one entry of the
 attribution table taken from 4 to 2 and back to 4, and the rule read nothing.
 THE MEND: each of the two ceilings is read along main's own line from the
 anchor: every state the file has stood in, in order, and the file as it is on
 the disk last. In the attribution table each step up of an entry needs a
 record of its own: one text under the entry's name pays for one step, and a
 list of texts for as many. For the identification baseline the steps up are
 added, and the raises recorded since the anchor must account for the whole.
 The gate's sentence names the commit in which each step stands. The rule of
 two ends is kept as it was and is asked first. WHAT IT COSTS, for main to
 weigh: a step that stands in a commit is not taken back by lowering the
 ceiling in a later commit. It is in the history, and it is recorded; the
 record can say that it was a mistake and where it was undone. So there is
 always a way to green that rewrites nothing. A step that is in no commit yet
 can still be lowered again, and the sentence says which kind it is. One
 assertion of the seat's own lock for series 2 held the old remedy, that a
 ceiling lowered after a committed rise reads as no rise; it now holds that
 the step is reported by its commit and passes once it is recorded, with a
 dated note. THIS TURNS ONE TEST OF MAIN'S LOCK FOR B1893 RED, AS ITS OWN NOTE
 SAYS IT WILL: test_a_ceiling_that_falls_and_rises_back_is_still_not_seen
 fails at its first assertion, where the plant on the identification baseline
 is now caught. The first assertion of its second pair cannot fail by any
 mend: it gives the rule of two states two equal tables, and two states hold
 no step between them. The case it means, 4 to 2 to 4, is caught where the
 history is read. The arc's page and its lock are main's to change. On main
 the attribution table has stood in 2 states since the anchor and the
 identification baseline in 1, with no step up; reading both histories takes
 four hundredths of a second, three calls of git for each. What it does not
 reach: a fall and a rise back that stayed on a side line and were merged as
 they began, which never moved the ceiling of main's line; a ceiling in any
 file but these two, which is the next patch. The lock: two tests added to
 tests/test_nothing_leaves_a_ledger.py. On the commit before, both fail, and
 the changed assertion with them: the gate says ok, and the rule reads
 nothing. Main's own script for the two cases, run on the mend: both plants
 are caught and both controls as before. Tested against planted errors;
 reproduced, main's two cases by main's own script; computed once. 0 of 19.

